window.LAB_ITEMS = {"version":1,"lab":4,"course":"Phonetics","title":"How Sounds Are Made","zodiac":{"index":4,"animal":"rabbit","label":"Rabbit"},"unit":{"one":"Trail","many":"trails"},"shuffle_items":true,"lede":"You will find the articulators for ten more sounds on the map of the mouth, work out which sound a picture shows from where it is made, how the air gets out and whether the voice is on, and build ten words in small families that differ by a single sound.","grading":{"total":30,"full_threshold":20,"full_score":100,"partial_score":50,"zero_score":0,"full_points":2,"partial_points":1,"alt_credit":0.5,"round":"up"},"legs":[{"id":"af","n":1,"title":"Articulator Finder","accent":"amber","subtitle":"Tap the articulators that make each sound","concept":"Same map as last week, ten new sounds. Most consonants use two <b>articulators</b>: a part that moves, usually the tongue, and the place it moves to. This week, also notice the <b>voice</b>. For a <b>voiced</b> sound such as /z/ the vocal folds vibrate while the mouth does its work; for a <b>voiceless</b> one such as /s/ they stay still. What you tap here are the parts that shape the sound; whether the voice is on is a clue you will read in Trail 2.","audio_note":"<b>Tap the sound to hear it.</b> A consonant is hard to hear on its own, so most are recorded with a vowel after: /m/ plays as <i>ma</i>. Listen to the front of the syllable.","prompt":"Tap every articulator this sound uses, then press Check.","regions":[{"v":"lips","label":"Lips"},{"v":"teeth","label":"Upper teeth"},{"v":"alveolar","label":"Alveolar ridge"},{"v":"postalv","label":"Postalveolar area"},{"v":"palate","label":"Hard palate"},{"v":"velum","label":"Velum"},{"v":"tip","label":"Tongue tip"},{"v":"blade","label":"Tongue blade"},{"v":"back","label":"Tongue back"},{"v":"glottis","label":"Vocal folds"}],"demo":{"steps":[{"ipa":"p","audio":"ipa_p","show":["lips"],"cap":"<b>/p/</b> uses the <b>lips</b>, and only the lips: they close, the air builds up behind them, and the sound is the release. One articulator. It is voiceless: the vocal folds stay still."},{"ipa":"f","audio":"ipa_f","show":["lips","teeth"],"cap":"<b>/f/</b> uses two: the <b>lower lip</b> is the part that moves, and the <b>upper teeth</b> are where it lands. Voiceless again; switch the voice on in the same position and it becomes /v/."},{"ipa":"g","audio":"ipa_g","show":["back","velum"],"cap":"<b>/g/</b> is made at the very back: the <b>back of the tongue</b> rises to the <b>velum</b>, the soft part of the roof of the mouth. Two again, and this one is voiced: the vocal folds vibrate."}]},"items":[{"id":"af01","type":"multispot","ipa":"m","answer":"lips","explain":"/m/ is made at the lips: the two lips close and stay closed, so it is <b>bilabial</b>, like /p/ and /b/. Two more things happen at the same time: the vocal folds vibrate, so /m/ is <b>voiced</b>, and the velum lowers so the air leaves through the nose, which is what makes it hum.","miss":{"lips.teeth":"Lip against teeth is the /f/ and /v/ position. For /m/ the upper lip meets the lower lip, and the teeth stay out of it.","alveolar.tip":"Tongue tip on the ridge is the other nasal, /n/. Say <i>mom</i> and watch a mirror: for /m/ the lips do all the work.","back.velum":"Tongue back against the velum is the nasal /ŋ/, as in <i>sing</i>. /m/ is sealed at the very front, by the lips.","lips.velum":"The velum does lower for /m/, to open the way into the nose, but the place where /m/ is made is the lips.","glottis.lips.velum":"The velum does lower for /m/, to open the way into the nose, but the place where /m/ is made is the lips."},"also":["glottis.lips"],"quiet":["glottis.lips"],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"af02","type":"multispot","ipa":"d","answer":"alveolar.tip","accept":{"alveolar":"Right place, but a place needs something to touch it. The tongue <b>tip</b> is what rises to the alveolar ridge for /d/; both parts are the answer.","tip":"Right mover. The tip has to land somewhere, and for /d/ it lands on the <b>alveolar ridge</b>, the bump just behind the upper teeth; both parts are the answer.","alveolar.glottis":"Right place, but a place needs something to touch it. The tongue <b>tip</b> is what rises to the alveolar ridge for /d/; both parts are the answer.","glottis.tip":"Right mover. The tip has to land somewhere, and for /d/ it lands on the <b>alveolar ridge</b>, the bump just behind the upper teeth; both parts are the answer."},"explain":"/d/ is an <b>alveolar</b> sound: the tongue tip presses against the alveolar ridge and blocks the air completely before letting it go. It is made exactly where /t/ is made; the difference is that for /d/ the vocal folds vibrate, so it is <b>voiced</b>.","miss":{"blade.postalv":"One step too far back. That area behind the ridge is where /ʃ/ and /dʒ/ live. /d/ is on the ridge itself, with the tip.","teeth.tip":"Tip to the teeth is /θ/ and /ð/, as in <i>this</i>. For /d/ the tip lands just behind the teeth, on the ridge."},"also":["alveolar.glottis.tip"],"quiet":["alveolar.glottis.tip"],"note":null},{"id":"af03","type":"multispot","ipa":"h","answer":"glottis","explain":"/h/ is the one English consonant made below the mouth. Nothing in the mouth touches anything; the only narrowing is at the <b>vocal folds</b>, which are held apart so that air rushes through the gap between them, the glottis. That makes /h/ a <b>glottal</b> sound, and because the folds are open rather than vibrating, a <b>voiceless</b> one.","miss":{"back.velum":"That is the /k/ and /g/ position, a full contact at the back of the mouth. For /h/ nothing in the mouth touches; the narrowing is lower down, at the vocal folds.","lips":"Say <i>he</i>, then <i>who</i>: the lips just take the shape of the vowel that follows. What makes the /h/ is air rushing between the vocal folds.","velum":"The velum is not where /h/ is made. Nothing in the mouth narrows for /h/; the air rushes through the open vocal folds."},"also":[],"quiet":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"af04","type":"multispot","ipa":"θ","answer":"teeth.tip","also":["teeth"],"accept":{"tip":"Right mover. For /θ/ the tip goes all the way forward to the <b>upper teeth</b>, not to the ridge behind them; the teeth are the place that names the sound.","glottis.teeth.tip":"Every part in the mouth is right. The slip is the <b>vocal folds</b>: /θ/ is <b>voiceless</b>, so they stay open and still while it is made. Put a hand on your throat and say it: no buzz.","glottis.teeth":"Every part in the mouth is right. The slip is the <b>vocal folds</b>: /θ/ is <b>voiceless</b>, so they stay open and still while it is made. Put a hand on your throat and say it: no buzz."},"explain":"/θ/, the first sound of <i>think</i>, is an <b>interdental</b> sound: the tongue tip touches the edge of the upper front teeth, or slips between the upper and lower teeth, and air hisses through the gap. It is <b>voiceless</b>: the vocal folds stay still. Naming the teeth alone, or teeth plus tongue tip, both count here.","miss":{"alveolar.tip":"That is one step back, on the ridge, where /s/ and /t/ are made. For /θ/ the tip comes all the way forward to the teeth.","lips.teeth":"Lip against teeth is /f/, which is easy to confuse with /θ/ by ear. For /θ/ it is the tongue, not the lip, at the teeth."},"quiet":[],"note":null},{"id":"af05","type":"multispot","ipa":"ŋ","answer":"back.velum","accept":{"velum":"Right place. The velum needs something to press against it, and for /ŋ/ that is the <b>back of the tongue</b>; both parts are the answer.","back":"Right mover. The back of the tongue rises for /ŋ/, and what it presses against is the <b>velum</b>, the soft palate at the back of the roof of the mouth; both parts are the answer.","glottis.velum":"Right place. The velum needs something to press against it, and for /ŋ/ that is the <b>back of the tongue</b>; both parts are the answer.","back.glottis":"Right mover. The back of the tongue rises for /ŋ/, and what it presses against is the <b>velum</b>, the soft palate at the back of the roof of the mouth; both parts are the answer."},"explain":"/ŋ/, the last sound of <i>sing</i>, is <b>velar</b>: the back of the tongue presses against the velum, the same contact as /k/ and /g/. It is <b>voiced</b>, and it is a nasal: with the mouth sealed at the back, the air goes out through the nose.","miss":{"alveolar.tip":"Tip on the ridge is the nasal /n/. Say <i>sin</i>, then <i>sing</i>: for the second one the tip stays down and the back of the tongue goes up.","back.palate":"Close, but the hard palate is the bony front part of the roof. /ŋ/ is made farther back, where the roof turns soft: the velum.","lips":"Lips together gives the nasal /m/. /ŋ/ is sealed at the other end of the mouth, tongue back against the velum."},"also":["back.glottis.velum"],"quiet":["back.glottis.velum"],"note":null},{"id":"af06","type":"multispot","ipa":"s","answer":"alveolar.tip","accept":{"alveolar":"Right place; add the mover. The <b>tongue tip</b> rises toward the ridge for /s/; both parts are the answer.","tip":"Right mover; add the place. For /s/ the tip rises to the <b>alveolar ridge</b>; both parts are the answer.","alveolar.glottis.tip":"Every part in the mouth is right. The slip is the <b>vocal folds</b>: /s/ is <b>voiceless</b>, so they stay open and still while it is made. Put a hand on your throat and say it: no buzz."},"explain":"/s/ is <b>alveolar</b>: the tongue tip rises to the alveolar ridge, the same spot as /t/ and /d/, but leaves a narrow groove for the air to hiss through. It is <b>voiceless</b>; switch the voice on in the same position and you get /z/.","miss":{"blade.postalv":"A little too far back: that is the /ʃ/ position. Say <i>see</i>, then <i>she</i>: for <i>see</i> the tongue is farther forward, tip at the ridge.","teeth.tip":"Tip to the teeth gives /θ/, as in <i>think</i>. /s/ sits just behind the teeth, on the ridge."},"also":[],"quiet":[],"note":null},{"id":"af07","type":"multispot","ipa":"ʒ","answer":"blade.postalv","accept":{"postalv":"Right place; add the mover. It is the <b>tongue blade</b>, the flat part just behind the tip, that rises there for /ʒ/. Both parts are the answer.","blade":"Right mover; add the place. The blade rises to the <b>postalveolar area</b>, just behind the ridge. Both parts are the answer.","glottis.postalv":"Right place; add the mover. It is the <b>tongue blade</b>, the flat part just behind the tip, that rises there for /ʒ/. Both parts are the answer.","blade.glottis":"Right mover; add the place. The blade rises to the <b>postalveolar area</b>, just behind the ridge. Both parts are the answer."},"explain":"/ʒ/, the middle sound of <i>measure</i>, is <b>postalveolar</b>: the tongue blade rises toward the roof of the mouth just behind the alveolar ridge, and air rushes through the gap. It is <b>voiced</b>; with the voice off, the same position gives /ʃ/.","miss":{"alveolar.tip":"That is the /z/ position. Say <i>zoo</i>, then the middle of <i>measure</i>: for /ʒ/ the tongue pulls back a little and its blade, not its tip, does the work.","blade.palate":"A little too far back. The hard palate is where /j/, as in <i>yes</i>, is made. /ʒ/ sits just behind the ridge."},"also":["blade.glottis.postalv"],"quiet":["blade.glottis.postalv"],"note":null},{"id":"af08","type":"multispot","ipa":"ɹ","answer":"postalv.tip","accept":{"postalv":"Right place; add the mover. For /ɹ/ it is the <b>tongue tip</b> that curls up toward the area just behind the ridge. Both parts are the answer.","tip":"Right mover; add the place. The tip curls up and back toward the <b>postalveolar area</b>, just behind the ridge, without touching it. Both parts are the answer.","blade.postalv":"The place is right. The part to name is the tongue <b>tip</b>, which curls up and back toward that area. Some speakers bunch the tongue instead, but the curled tip is the usual description.","glottis.postalv":"Right place; add the mover. For /ɹ/ it is the <b>tongue tip</b> that curls up toward the area just behind the ridge. Both parts are the answer.","glottis.tip":"Right mover; add the place. The tip curls up and back toward the <b>postalveolar area</b>, just behind the ridge, without touching it. Both parts are the answer.","blade.glottis.postalv":"The place is right. The part to name is the tongue <b>tip</b>, which curls up and back toward that area. Some speakers bunch the tongue instead, but the curled tip is the usual description."},"explain":"/ɹ/, the first sound of <i>red</i>, is <b>postalveolar</b>: the tongue tip curls up and a little back, toward the area just behind the alveolar ridge, and touches nothing; the air glides past it. It is <b>voiced</b>.","miss":{"alveolar.tip":"Right mover, but on the ridge the tip makes /l/ or /d/. For /ɹ/ it curls a little farther back and never touches.","lips":"The lips often round for /ɹ/, which helps explain why young children may say <i>wed</i> for <i>red</i>, but what makes the sound is the tongue."},"also":["glottis.postalv.tip"],"quiet":["glottis.postalv.tip"],"note":null},{"id":"af09","type":"multispot","ipa":"l","answer":"alveolar.tip","accept":{"alveolar":"Right place; add the mover. The <b>tongue tip</b> is what touches the ridge for /l/; both parts are the answer.","tip":"Right mover; add the place. For /l/ the tip presses on the <b>alveolar ridge</b>; both parts are the answer.","alveolar.glottis":"Right place; add the mover. The <b>tongue tip</b> is what touches the ridge for /l/; both parts are the answer.","glottis.tip":"Right mover; add the place. For /l/ the tip presses on the <b>alveolar ridge</b>; both parts are the answer."},"explain":"/l/ is <b>alveolar</b>: the tongue tip presses on the alveolar ridge, as for /d/, but the sides of the tongue stay down, so the air flows around the contact and out. It is <b>voiced</b>. Hold the /l/ of <i>light</i> and breathe in: you will feel cool air on the sides of the tongue.","miss":{"postalv.tip":"One step too far back: tip toward the area behind the ridge is /ɹ/. For /l/ the tip lands on the ridge itself.","teeth.tip":"Tip to the teeth is /θ/ and /ð/. For /l/ the tip lands just behind the teeth, on the ridge."},"also":["alveolar.glottis.tip"],"quiet":["alveolar.glottis.tip"],"note":null},{"id":"af10","type":"multispot","ipa":"tʃ","answer":"blade.postalv","accept":{"postalv":"Right place; add the mover. The <b>tongue blade</b> makes the contact for /tʃ/; both parts are the answer.","blade":"Right mover; add the place. The blade goes to the <b>postalveolar area</b>, just behind the ridge, the same spot as /ʃ/; both parts are the answer.","blade.glottis.postalv":"Every part in the mouth is right. The slip is the <b>vocal folds</b>: /tʃ/ is <b>voiceless</b>, so they stay open and still while it is made. Put a hand on your throat and say it: no buzz."},"explain":"/tʃ/, the first sound of <i>chair</i>, is <b>postalveolar</b>: tongue blade up just behind the alveolar ridge. It starts with a full closure, like /t/, and releases into a /ʃ/-like hiss. It is <b>voiceless</b>; its voiced twin is /dʒ/, as in <i>jump</i>.","miss":{"alveolar.tip":"That is the /t/ spot, and /tʃ/ does start like a /t/, but pulled back a step: blade, not tip, just behind the ridge.","back.velum":"The velum is the /k/ and /g/ position. /tʃ/ is made much farther forward, just behind the ridge."},"also":[],"quiet":[],"note":null}]},{"id":"sd","n":2,"title":"Sound Detective","accent":"indigo","subtitle":"Work out which sound the picture shows","concept":"A consonant can be described by three facts: <b>where</b> it is made, which articulators touch or come close; <b>how</b> the air gets out, blocked, squeezed through a gap, or gliding freely; and whether the <b>vocal folds vibrate</b> while it happens. This week brings more sounds where the air <b>glides</b> out with no friction at all, and two new kinds of <i>how</i>: one where the air flows <b>around the sides</b> of the tongue, and one where it is <b>blocked, then let go into a hiss</b>.","prompt":"Read the picture, then tap the sound it shows.","tiles":[{"ipa":"w","slug":"w"},{"ipa":"ð","slug":"dh"},{"ipa":"t","slug":"t"},{"ipa":"z","slug":"z"},{"ipa":"v","slug":"v"},{"ipa":"l","slug":"l"},{"ipa":"ɹ","slug":"r"},{"ipa":"j","slug":"y"},{"ipa":"tʃ","slug":"ch"},{"ipa":"ʃ","slug":"sh"}],"demo":{"audio":"ipa_n","panel":{"show":["tip","alveolar"],"flow":"nasal","place":"Tongue tip against the alveolar ridge","manner":"The mouth is sealed; air flows out through the nose","voiced":true},"clues":[{"k":"where","btn":"Where?","cap":"<b>Where:</b> the lit parts are the tongue tip and the alveolar ridge. A lot of sounds are made there: /t/, /d/, /s/, /z/, /n/ and /l/."},{"k":"how","btn":"How?","cap":"<b>How:</b> the cross means the mouth is sealed at the ridge, and the dotted line shows the air leaving through the nose instead. Only one of those six sends its air through the nose."},{"k":"voice","btn":"Voice?","cap":"<b>Voice:</b> the badge says the vocal folds vibrate, which fits. <b>The sound is /n/.</b>"}]},"items":[{"id":"sd01","type":"pick","answer":"w","panel":{"show":["lips","back","velum"],"flow":"approx","place":"Lips rounded; back of the tongue raised toward the velum","manner":"A wide gap; air glides through without friction","voiced":true,"at":["lips"]},"explain":"Two places at once, rounded lips and a raised tongue back, with the air gliding through and the voice on: that is /w/, the first sound of <i>we</i>. It is the only English consonant that lights up both ends of the mouth.","miss":{"v":"/v/ is a lip sound too, but the lower lip touches the upper teeth and the air hisses. Here the lips round without touching anything, and there is no friction.","y":"/j/ glides like this, but its tongue is raised at the front, toward the hard palate, and the lips are not rounded.","r":"/ɹ/ often has rounded lips too, but its tongue tip curls up behind the ridge. Here it is the back of the tongue that is raised."},"also":[],"quiet":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"sd02","type":"pick","answer":"dh","panel":{"show":["tip","teeth"],"flow":"fric","place":"Tongue tip between the teeth","manner":"A narrow gap; air hisses through","voiced":true},"explain":"Tongue tip at the teeth, air hissing, and the voice on: that is /ð/, the first sound of <i>this</i>. With the voice off, the same position gives /θ/, as in <i>think</i>.","miss":{"z":"/z/ is a voiced hiss too, but it is made a step farther back, tip toward the ridge, not between the teeth.","v":"/v/ is lip against teeth. Here it is the tongue tip at the teeth."},"also":[],"quiet":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"sd03","type":"pick","answer":"t","panel":{"show":["tip","alveolar"],"flow":"stop","place":"Tongue tip against the alveolar ridge","manner":"Air is blocked completely, then released in a burst","voiced":false},"explain":"Tip on the ridge, a full block, and no voice: that is /t/. Its voiced twin /d/ is made in exactly the same place; the silent vocal folds are what make this one /t/.","miss":{"z":"/z/ is made in the same place, but with a narrow groove for the air to hiss through, and with voice. This panel shows a full block and no voice.","l":"/l/ puts the tip on the ridge too, but lets the air flow around the sides and is voiced. This panel shows a full block and no voice.","ch":"/tʃ/ starts with a block like this one, but it is made a step farther back with the blade, and lets go into a hiss rather than a clean burst."},"also":[],"quiet":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"sd04","type":"pick","answer":"z","panel":{"show":["tip","alveolar"],"flow":"fric","place":"Tongue tip raised toward the alveolar ridge","manner":"A narrow groove; air hisses through","voiced":true},"explain":"Tip toward the ridge, a narrow groove, air hissing, voice on: that is /z/. Same place as /t/, but the air squeezes through instead of being blocked; with the voice off it would be /s/.","miss":{"dh":"/ð/ is a voiced hiss too, but the tip is between the teeth, farther forward than the ridge shown here.","sh":"/ʃ/ hisses, but it is made a step farther back with the blade, and it has no voice. This panel shows the tip at the ridge with the voice on.","t":"/t/ is made in the same place, but it blocks the air completely and has no voice. This panel shows a hiss with the voice on."},"also":[],"quiet":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"sd05","type":"pick","answer":"v","panel":{"show":["lips","teeth"],"flow":"fric","place":"Lower lip against the upper teeth","manner":"A narrow gap; air hisses through","voiced":true},"explain":"Lower lip touching the upper teeth, air hissing through, voice on: that is /v/, a <b>labiodental</b> sound. Its voiceless twin is /f/.","miss":{"w":"/w/ uses the lips too, but they round without touching anything and the air glides out smoothly. Here the lip touches the teeth and the air hisses.","dh":"/ð/ is tongue tip between the teeth. Here it is the lip at the teeth, not the tongue."},"also":[],"quiet":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"sd06","type":"pick","answer":"l","panel":{"show":["tip","alveolar"],"flow":"lateral","place":"Tongue tip against the alveolar ridge","manner":"Sealed down the middle; air flows smoothly around the sides of the tongue","voiced":true},"explain":"Tip on the ridge, but the air is not stopped: it slips around the lowered sides of the tongue, with the voice on. That is /l/, the only English sound that sends its air around the sides, which is why it is called a <b>lateral</b>.","miss":{"t":"/t/ puts the tip in the same place, but seals the mouth completely and has no voice. Here the air keeps flowing, around the sides.","r":"/ɹ/ is close to /l/, but its tip touches nothing and sits a step farther back. This panel shows the tip pressed on the ridge.","z":"/z/ is made at the ridge with voice, but its air hisses down the middle through a narrow groove. Here the middle is sealed and the air goes around."},"also":[],"quiet":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"sd07","type":"pick","answer":"r","panel":{"show":["tip","postalv"],"flow":"approx","place":"Tongue tip curled up toward the area just behind the ridge, touching nothing","manner":"A wide gap; air glides through without friction","voiced":true},"explain":"Tip curled up behind the ridge without touching, a gap too wide for any hiss, voice on: that is /ɹ/, the first sound of <i>red</i>.","miss":{"l":"/l/ is close, but its tip presses on the ridge and the air goes around the sides. Here nothing touches.","y":"/j/ glides like this, but it is made with the front of the tongue up near the hard palate, not with the tip behind the ridge.","w":"/w/ glides like this, but it is made with rounded lips and a raised tongue back. This panel lights the tongue tip."},"also":[],"quiet":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"sd08","type":"pick","answer":"y","panel":{"show":["blade","palate"],"flow":"approx","place":"Front of the tongue raised toward the hard palate","manner":"A wide gap; air glides through without friction","voiced":true},"explain":"Tongue front toward the hard palate, a gap too wide for any hiss, voice on: that is /j/, the first sound of <i>yes</i>. Its symbol is the letter j, but it is the <i>y</i> sound.","miss":{"sh":"/ʃ/ is close by, but its gap is narrow enough to hiss and it has no voice. This panel shows a wide gap, smooth airflow, and the voice on.","r":"/ɹ/ glides like this, but with the tongue tip curled up behind the ridge. Here it is the front of the tongue, up near the hard palate.","w":"/w/ glides like this, but with rounded lips and the tongue raised at the back."},"also":[],"quiet":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"sd09","type":"pick","answer":"ch","panel":{"show":["blade","postalv"],"flow":"affric","place":"Tongue blade against the area just behind the alveolar ridge","manner":"Air is blocked completely, then let go slowly into a hiss","voiced":false},"accept":{"sh":"You read the place, the hiss and the missing voice correctly. What separates them is the start: this panel shows the air <b>blocked completely first</b>, and /ʃ/ never blocks it."},"explain":"Blade just behind the ridge, a full block that lets go into a hiss, no voice: that is /tʃ/, the first sound of <i>chair</i>. A block and a hiss fused into one sound like this is called an <b>affricate</b>.","miss":{"t":"/t/ blocks the air too, but at the ridge with the tip, and it lets go in a clean burst. This panel is a step farther back, with the blade, and ends in a hiss."},"also":[],"quiet":[],"note":null},{"id":"sd10","type":"pick","answer":"sh","panel":{"show":["blade","postalv"],"flow":"fric","place":"Tongue blade raised just behind the alveolar ridge","manner":"A narrow gap; air rushes through with friction","voiced":false},"accept":{"ch":"You read the place, the hiss and the missing voice correctly. But /tʃ/ starts by <b>blocking the air completely</b>, and this panel shows the air rushing through from the start."},"explain":"Blade just behind the ridge, air rushing through, no voice: that is /ʃ/, the first sound of <i>shoe</i>. Its voiced twin is /ʒ/.","miss":{"z":"/z/ hisses too, but it is made a step farther forward, tip at the ridge, and with the voice on. This panel shows the blade behind the ridge and no voice.","y":"/j/ is close by, but its gap is wide enough that there is no friction, and it is voiced."},"also":[],"quiet":[],"note":null}]},{"id":"wb","n":3,"title":"Word Builder","accent":"magenta","subtitle":"Build each word out of its sounds","concept":"Every spoken word is a chain of phonemes. Listen, then stamp the sounds into the boxes in order. The number of boxes tells you how many sounds the word has; your job is which sounds, in which order. This week's words come in families that differ by a single sound, <i>bit, beat, bet, bat</i>, so the work is in hearing exactly which vowel, or which first consonant, you are listening to.","audio_note":"<b>Tap any tile to hear its sound.</b> Consonants are recorded with a vowel after, /b/ as <i>ba</i>, so listen to the front of the syllable.","prompt":"Tap the sounds in order to build the word. Tap a filled box to take one out.","tiles":[{"ipa":"b","slug":"b","cat":"c"},{"ipa":"t","slug":"t","cat":"c"},{"ipa":"d","slug":"d","cat":"c"},{"ipa":"k","slug":"k","cat":"c"},{"ipa":"m","slug":"m","cat":"c"},{"ipa":"ɹ","slug":"r","cat":"c"},{"ipa":"s","slug":"s","cat":"c"},{"ipa":"θ","slug":"th","cat":"c"},{"ipa":"ð","slug":"dh","cat":"c"},{"ipa":"dʒ","slug":"jh","cat":"c"},{"ipa":"i","slug":"iy","cat":"v"},{"ipa":"ɪ","slug":"ih","cat":"v"},{"ipa":"eɪ","slug":"ey","cat":"v"},{"ipa":"ɛ","slug":"eh","cat":"v"},{"ipa":"æ","slug":"ae","cat":"v"},{"ipa":"ʌ","slug":"ah","cat":"v"},{"ipa":"ɑ","slug":"aa","cat":"v"},{"ipa":"ɑɪ","slug":"ay","cat":"v"},{"ipa":"ɑʊ","slug":"aw","cat":"v"},{"ipa":"oʊ","slug":"ow","cat":"v"}],"demo":{"word":"cat","audio":"w_cat","want":["k","ae","t"],"tiles":[{"ipa":"k","slug":"k"},{"ipa":"æ","slug":"ae"},{"ipa":"ɛ","slug":"eh"},{"ipa":"t","slug":"t"}],"cap":"<b>cat</b> has three letters and three sounds. The letter <b>c</b> spells the sound /k/. For the vowel, choose between /æ/ and /ɛ/ by ear. Tap the sounds in order. Tap a filled box to take it back out.","ok":"<b>/k/ + /æ/ + /t/ = cat.</b> With /ɛ/ in the middle it would be <i>ket</i>, which is not a word; one vowel is all that separates them."},"items":[{"id":"wb01","type":"build","word":"bit","audio":"w_bit","answer":"b.ih.t","chips":[{"letters":"b","ipa":"b","slug":"b"},{"letters":"i","ipa":"ɪ","slug":"ih"},{"letters":"t","ipa":"t","slug":"t"}],"explain":"Three letters, three sounds. The vowel is the short, relaxed /ɪ/. <i>Bit</i>, <i>beat</i>, <i>bet</i> and <i>bat</i> share the /b/ and the /t/ and differ only in the vowel, so listen to the middle.","miss":{"b.iy.t":"/i/ is the long, tight vowel of <i>beat</i>. <i>Bit</i> has the short, relaxed /ɪ/. The two words differ by exactly that vowel.","b.eh.t":"/ɛ/ is the vowel of <i>bet</i>, made with the jaw a little more open. <i>Bit</i> has /ɪ/."},"also":[],"quiet":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"wb02","type":"build","word":"beat","audio":"w_beat","answer":"b.iy.t","chips":[{"letters":"b","ipa":"b","slug":"b"},{"letters":"ea","ipa":"i","slug":"iy"},{"letters":"t","ipa":"t","slug":"t"}],"explain":"Four letters, three sounds: the pair <b>ea</b> spells one vowel, the long, tight /i/ of <i>see</i>. <i>Bit</i> and <i>beat</i> are a minimal pair.","miss":{"b.ih.t":"/ɪ/ is the vowel of <i>bit</i>. <i>Beat</i> is longer and tighter: /i/, the vowel of <i>see</i>.","b.ey.t":"The letters <b>ea</b> do spell /eɪ/ in <i>great</i>, but in <i>beat</i> they spell /i/. /b/ /eɪ/ /t/ would be <i>bait</i>."},"also":[],"quiet":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"wb03","type":"build","word":"bet","audio":"w_bet","answer":"b.eh.t","chips":[{"letters":"b","ipa":"b","slug":"b"},{"letters":"e","ipa":"ɛ","slug":"eh"},{"letters":"t","ipa":"t","slug":"t"}],"explain":"Three letters, three sounds. The vowel is /ɛ/, the vowel of <i>bed</i>: the jaw is a little more open than for the /ɪ/ of <i>bit</i>.","miss":{"b.ae.t":"/æ/ is the vowel of <i>bat</i>, with the jaw lower still. <i>Bet</i> has /ɛ/, the vowel of <i>bed</i>.","b.ey.t":"/b/ /eɪ/ /t/ is <i>bait</i>. The letter <b>e</b> in <i>bet</i> is the short vowel /ɛ/, with no glide.","b.ih.t":"/ɪ/ is the vowel of <i>bit</i>. For <i>bet</i> the jaw opens a little more: /ɛ/."},"also":[],"quiet":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"wb04","type":"build","word":"bat","audio":"w_bat","answer":"b.ae.t","chips":[{"letters":"b","ipa":"b","slug":"b"},{"letters":"a","ipa":"æ","slug":"ae"},{"letters":"t","ipa":"t","slug":"t"}],"explain":"Three letters, three sounds. The vowel is /æ/, the most open of the four: say <i>beat, bit, bet, bat</i> in a row and feel the jaw drop one step each time.","miss":{"b.eh.t":"/ɛ/ is the vowel of <i>bet</i>. For <i>bat</i> the jaw drops one more step: /æ/.","b.aa.t":"/ɑ/ is the vowel of <i>hot</i>, made at the back of the mouth. The <b>a</b> of <i>bat</i> is the front vowel /æ/.","b.ah.t":"/b/ /ʌ/ /t/ is <i>but</i>. <i>Bat</i> has the front vowel /æ/."},"also":[],"quiet":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"wb05","type":"build","word":"jam","audio":"w_jam","answer":"jh.ae.m","chips":[{"letters":"j","ipa":"dʒ","slug":"jh"},{"letters":"a","ipa":"æ","slug":"ae"},{"letters":"m","ipa":"m","slug":"m"}],"accept":{"jh.eh.m":"Before /m/, the American /æ/ is said with the tongue a little higher, so it can sound close to /ɛ/. It is still the /æ/ of <i>bat</i>: compare <i>jam</i> with <i>gem</i>, which does have /ɛ/."},"explain":"Three letters, three sounds. The letter <b>j</b> spells the single sound /dʒ/, and the vowel is the /æ/ of <i>bat</i>.","miss":{"d.ae.m":"/d/ /æ/ /m/ is <i>dam</i>. The letter <b>j</b> spells /dʒ/, which starts like a /d/ but lets go into a hiss. It has its own tile."},"also":[],"quiet":[],"note":null},{"id":"wb06","type":"build","word":"ram","audio":"w_ram","answer":"r.ae.m","chips":[{"letters":"r","ipa":"ɹ","slug":"r"},{"letters":"a","ipa":"æ","slug":"ae"},{"letters":"m","ipa":"m","slug":"m"}],"accept":{"r.eh.m":"Before /m/, the American /æ/ is said with the tongue a little higher, so it can sound close to /ɛ/. It is still the /æ/ of <i>bat</i>: compare <i>ram</i> with <i>hem</i>, which does have /ɛ/."},"explain":"Three letters, three sounds. The symbol for the English <i>r</i> sound is the upside-down /ɹ/. <i>Jam</i> and <i>ram</i> are a minimal pair: only the first sound changes.","miss":{"r.aa.m":"/ɑ/ is the back vowel of <i>hot</i>. The <b>a</b> of <i>ram</i> is the front vowel /æ/, as in <i>bat</i>.","r.ah.m":"/ɹ/ /ʌ/ /m/ is <i>rum</i>. <i>Ram</i> has the front vowel /æ/."},"also":[],"quiet":[],"note":null},{"id":"wb07","type":"build","word":"bye","audio":"w_bye","answer":"b.ay","chips":[{"letters":"b","ipa":"b","slug":"b"},{"letters":"ye","ipa":"ɑɪ","slug":"ay"}],"explain":"Three letters, two sounds. <b>ye</b> spells one gliding vowel, /ɑɪ/, which starts open and rises toward /ɪ/. A gliding vowel like this counts as a single sound.","miss":{"b.ey":"/b/ /eɪ/ is <i>bay</i>. <i>Bye</i> starts with the mouth much more open: /ɑɪ/, the vowel of <i>my</i>.","b.iy":"/b/ /i/ is <i>bee</i>. The vowel of <i>bye</i> is the gliding vowel /ɑɪ/, as in <i>my</i>.","b.aa":"That is only where it starts: /ɑɪ/ begins near /ɑ/, then glides upward, and the glide is part of the sound. It has its own tile."},"also":[],"quiet":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"wb08","type":"build","word":"bay","audio":"w_bay","answer":"b.ey","chips":[{"letters":"b","ipa":"b","slug":"b"},{"letters":"ay","ipa":"eɪ","slug":"ey"}],"explain":"Three letters, two sounds. <b>ay</b> spells the gliding vowel /eɪ/, as in <i>say</i>. <i>Bye</i> and <i>bay</i> are a minimal pair.","miss":{"b.ay":"The spelling is a trap: /ɑɪ/ is the vowel of <i>bye</i>. The letters <b>ay</b> in <i>bay</i> spell /eɪ/, the vowel of <i>say</i>.","b.eh":"That is only where it starts: /eɪ/ begins near /ɛ/, then glides upward, and the glide is part of the sound. It has its own tile.","b.iy":"/b/ /i/ is <i>bee</i>. <i>Bay</i> has the gliding vowel /eɪ/."},"also":[],"quiet":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"wb09","type":"build","word":"thick","audio":"w_thick","answer":"th.ih.k","chips":[{"letters":"th","ipa":"θ","slug":"th"},{"letters":"i","ipa":"ɪ","slug":"ih"},{"letters":"ck","ipa":"k","slug":"k"}],"explain":"Five letters, three sounds. <b>th</b> spells the single voiceless sound /θ/, and <b>ck</b> spells a single /k/.","miss":{"dh.ih.k":"Right place, wrong voice. /ð/ is the voiced <b>th</b> of <i>this</i>. <i>Thick</i> starts with the voiceless one, /θ/, as in <i>think</i>: hand on your throat, there is no buzz.","t.ih.k":"/t/ /ɪ/ /k/ is <i>tick</i>. The letters <b>th</b> spell one sound, /θ/, made with the tongue tip at the teeth.","s.ih.k":"/s/ /ɪ/ /k/ is <i>sick</i>. <i>Thick</i> starts with /θ/, tongue tip at the teeth rather than at the ridge."},"also":[],"quiet":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"wb10","type":"build","word":"sick","audio":"w_sick","answer":"s.ih.k","chips":[{"letters":"s","ipa":"s","slug":"s"},{"letters":"i","ipa":"ɪ","slug":"ih"},{"letters":"ck","ipa":"k","slug":"k"}],"explain":"Four letters, three sounds. <i>Thick</i> and <i>sick</i> are a minimal pair: /θ/ with the tip at the teeth, /s/ with the tip at the ridge, and everything after that the same.","miss":{"th.ih.k":"/θ/ /ɪ/ /k/ is <i>thick</i>. <i>Sick</i> starts with /s/, made a step farther back, tip at the ridge.","s.iy.k":"/s/ /i/ /k/ is <i>seek</i>. <i>Sick</i> has the short, relaxed /ɪ/."},"also":[],"quiet":[],"accept":{},"note":null}]}]};
